JRBrown wrote:
> 
> There seem to be two core reasons why people dislike pudding farming:
>      1. Farming is a profitable activity that can be engaged in with
> minimal thought and even more minimal effort (on most platforms) by
> repeating a short command sequence 60,000 times.
>      2. Farming leads to unlimited items.
> 
> I agree wholeheartedly that reason one is a bad thing and should be
> changed. I disagree vehemently that reason two is a bad thing.

I see one problem in the fact that 1. and 2. are coupled and can not
be viewed isolated in the current implementation.

We already have quasi unlimited items, as many as necessary, usually
obtainable by somewhat more risk and diversity of play than you get
by pudding farming. (See the respective postings about the theme of
"statistics, the practical and theoretical" where that's discussed.)

> If someone decides that there should not be more than a "reasonable"
> quantity of items available, the next question of course is what is a
> "reasonable" quantity?

...therefore I cannot agree to that; that question seems meaningless
in practice.

> [...] I feel strongly that the
> *possibility* of being able to acquire "huge piles of loot" is a
> desirable quality of the game and should be preserved. I just think it
> should be made less automatable.

This part sounds like we not much disagree. But I fear that you still
assume that an unbalanced pudding farming feature would be required for
that; and here we disagree.

